1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,495
这是一道考题
This is an exam question.

2
00:00:02,545 --> 00:00:04,655
这是 AI 交上来的答卷
And this is the answer an AI handed in.

3
00:00:04,675 --> 00:00:07,440
过去一段时间，每个新模型出来
For a while now, every time a new model comes out,

4
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:11,265
都有人先让它画一只骑自行车的鹈鹕
someone first asks it to draw a pelican riding a bicycle.

5
00:00:11,285 --> 00:00:13,780
看多了，我的反应只剩：哦
After seeing so many, all I can say is: oh,

6
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:15,475
又一只鹈鹕
another pelican.

7
00:00:15,495 --> 00:00:17,775
直到我想：要是让这只鹈鹕
Until I wondered: what if this pelican

8
00:00:17,795 --> 00:00:20,980
穿越整个人类画过的画呢？
rode through every painting humans have ever made?

9
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:46,970
你可能第一反应：又是 AI 生成的视频
Your first thought might be: another AI-generated video.

10
00:00:46,990 --> 00:00:49,710
是也不是。刚才那二十个时代
Yes and no. In those twenty eras you just saw,

11
00:00:49,730 --> 00:00:51,250
没有一帧是生成的
not a single frame was generated.

12
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:54,830
是 AI 一行一行写出来的代码
It is code, written by an AI line by line.

13
00:00:54,850 --> 00:00:56,805
不信？我改一个数字
Don't believe me? I'll change one number.

14
00:00:58,390 --> 00:01:00,545
欸？……好像改大了
Huh? ...Maybe a bit too much.

15
00:01:01,245 --> 00:01:03,175
一会代码我会全部开源
I will open-source all of the code,

16
00:01:03,195 --> 00:01:06,515
还送上本视频的SKILL
along with this video's SKILL.

17
00:01:06,535 --> 00:01:08,235
地址在简介区
The link is in the description.

18
00:01:08,435 --> 00:01:10,460
可做这部片子的时候，我一直在想
But while making this film, I kept wondering:

19
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:14,505
为什么机器画的东西，总看着有点假？
why do machine-made images always look a little fake?

20
00:01:14,525 --> 00:01:17,275
更怪的是，写这些代码的 AI
Stranger still, the AI that wrote this code

21
00:01:17,295 --> 00:01:20,085
从来没看过自己的画面动起来
has never seen its own images move.

22
00:01:31,185 --> 00:01:32,435
1829 年
1829.

23
00:01:32,455 --> 00:01:35,215
比利时一个叫普拉托的年轻物理学家
A young Belgian physicist named Plateau,

24
00:01:35,235 --> 00:01:37,715
为了研究光在眼睛里留下的残影
studying the afterimages light leaves in the eye,

25
00:01:37,735 --> 00:01:40,745
盯着太阳看了 25 秒
stared at the sun for 25 seconds.

26
00:01:40,945 --> 00:01:42,805
三年后，他做出一个纸盘
Three years later, he made a paper disc

27
00:01:42,825 --> 00:01:46,525
边上开一圈缝，对着镜子转
with slits around the edge, spun in front of a mirror,

28
00:01:46,545 --> 00:01:48,555
盘上的画就动了
and the pictures on it moved.

29
00:01:50,255 --> 00:01:53,145
又过了十一年，他完全失明了
Eleven years after that, he went completely blind.

30
00:01:53,165 --> 00:01:56,495
据记载，可能和那次盯着太阳有关
Records suggest it may have been from staring at the sun.

31
00:01:56,515 --> 00:01:58,715
之后他靠儿子和女婿的眼睛
After that, through the eyes of his son and son-in-law,

32
00:01:58,735 --> 00:02:00,885
继续做研究
he carried on his research.

33
00:02:01,085 --> 00:02:04,195
让画动起来的人，最后看不见画了
The man who made pictures move could no longer see them.

34
00:02:04,295 --> 00:02:06,645
记住这个人，一会要考
Remember him. He'll be on the test.

35
00:02:38,745 --> 00:02:42,210
这是梅里爱自己讲的故事，真假难辨
That's Méliès's own story; true or not, no one can say.

36
00:02:42,230 --> 00:02:45,755
但从那以后，电影学会了变魔术
But from then on, film learned to do magic.

37
00:02:58,055 --> 00:02:59,660
一个黄昏
One evening at dusk,

38
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:02,000
康定斯基回到慕尼黑的画室
Kandinsky returned to his studio in Munich

39
00:03:02,020 --> 00:03:04,800
看见墙边有一幅美爆了的画
and saw a stunningly beautiful painting by the wall.

40
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:08,025
走近才认出来：那是他自己的画
Up close, he recognized it: it was his own painting,

41
00:03:08,045 --> 00:03:10,390
只是侧着靠在了墙上
just leaning on its side against the wall.

42
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:16,030
他后来写道：从那天起他确定
He later wrote that from that day he was certain

43
00:03:16,050 --> 00:03:18,675
画里的物体，伤害了他的画
objects were harming his paintings.

44
00:03:18,775 --> 00:03:21,405
十多年后，柏林的银幕上
A decade or so later, on a Berlin screen,

45
00:03:21,425 --> 00:03:25,275
出现了一部只有形状和颜色在动的电影
appeared a film where only shapes and colors moved.

46
00:03:44,775 --> 00:03:46,380
1935 年
1935.

47
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:50,695
新西兰人莱恩·莱要给英国邮政做一条广告
New Zealander Len Lye was to make an ad for the British Post Office.

48
00:03:50,715 --> 00:03:53,035
据新西兰传记辞典记载
According to the Dictionary of New Zealand Biography,

49
00:03:53,055 --> 00:03:55,445
他买不起摄影机
he couldn't afford a camera.

50
00:03:55,465 --> 00:03:59,120
于是他干脆不拍了，直接在胶片上画
So he stopped filming and painted straight onto the film.

51
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:51,280
第二次世界大战后
After World War II,

52
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:54,640
美国留下了大批高射炮的指挥仪
America was left with piles of anti-aircraft gun directors,

53
00:04:54,660 --> 00:04:58,280
一种靠齿轮和凸轮做计算的机器
machines that calculated with gears and cams.

54
00:04:58,300 --> 00:05:00,335
约翰·惠特尼把一台指挥仪
John Whitney took one of these directors

55
00:05:00,355 --> 00:05:03,030
改装成了画画的机器
and turned it into a drawing machine.

56
00:05:03,050 --> 00:05:04,195
1958 年
1958.

57
00:05:04,215 --> 00:05:05,995
他用它把螺旋做成了动画
He used it to animate spirals

58
00:05:06,015 --> 00:05:09,320
帮《迷魂记》做了片头
for the title sequence of Vertigo.

59
00:05:09,340 --> 00:05:10,955
这台机器本来是用来计算
The machine was built to calculate

60
00:05:10,975 --> 00:05:13,570
炮弹的射击弧线轨道
the arcs of artillery shells.

61
00:05:13,590 --> 00:05:17,490
惠特尼看见的是：弧线本身，就是画
What Whitney saw: the arc itself was the picture.

62
00:05:17,510 --> 00:05:18,780
1961 年
1961.

63
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:21,200
他把它能画的东西剪成一部短片
He cut what it could draw into a short film

64
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:22,710
叫《目录》
called Catalog.

65
00:05:56,710 --> 00:05:59,875
1981 年，纽约郊外有一家公司
In 1981, a company outside New York

66
00:05:59,895 --> 00:06:00,715
在给迪士尼的
was making computer graphics for Disney's

67
00:06:00,735 --> 00:06:03,840
《电子世界争霸战》做电脑画面
Tron.

68
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:07,365
程序员肯·佩林后来回忆：那时候
Programmer Ken Perlin later recalled: back then,

69
00:06:07,385 --> 00:06:09,800
一切看起来都像机器
everything looked machine-like.

70
00:06:09,900 --> 00:06:12,750
当时大多数人都想把画面做得更光滑
Most people wanted images smoother,

71
00:06:12,770 --> 00:06:14,490
更精确
more precise.

72
00:06:14,510 --> 00:06:18,070
他反而觉得，问题就出在太精确
He thought the problem was that they were too precise.

73
00:06:29,570 --> 00:06:32,380
1983 年，他写出一个函数
In 1983, he wrote a function:

74
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:34,450
随机，但是连续
random, but continuous.

75
00:07:09,950 --> 00:07:11,305
1985 年
1985.

76
00:07:11,325 --> 00:07:13,765
他把这套方法发表了出来
He published the method.

77
00:07:13,785 --> 00:07:15,135
1997 年
1997.

78
00:07:15,155 --> 00:07:17,790
它拿到了奥斯卡技术成就奖
It won an Academy Award for Technical Achievement.

79
00:07:57,290 --> 00:07:59,915
电脑画面的假，问题不是不够像
Computer images look fake not because they aren't realistic enough,

80
00:07:59,935 --> 00:08:02,055
而是太干净了
but because they are too clean.

81
00:08:02,075 --> 00:08:05,305
四十年后，这个问题又回到了原点
Forty years later, the problem is back where it started.

82
00:08:11,305 --> 00:08:12,450
1980 年代
The 1980s.

83
00:08:12,470 --> 00:08:14,930
给游戏去掉复制保护的少年们
Teenagers who cracked copy protection on games

84
00:08:14,950 --> 00:08:18,310
喜欢在游戏开头加一段带音乐的动画
liked to add an animation with music at the start

85
00:08:18,330 --> 00:08:20,640
当作自己的签名
as their signature.

86
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:22,460
后来，这些签名脱离了游戏
Later, these signatures broke away from the games

87
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:25,480
变成一种比赛：谁能在 64KB
and became a competition: who could fit, in 64KB

88
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:29,215
甚至 4KB 里，塞进最多的画面
or even 4KB, the most visuals.

89
00:08:29,235 --> 00:08:32,485
2020 年起，好几个欧洲国家
Since 2020, several European countries

90
00:08:32,505 --> 00:08:36,465
把它列进了国家级非物质文化遗产名录
have listed it as national intangible cultural heritage.

91
00:09:12,565 --> 00:09:14,545
2009 年，一部 4KB 的
In 2009, a 4KB

92
00:09:14,565 --> 00:09:17,025
作品《Elevated》
production called Elevated

93
00:09:17,045 --> 00:09:18,705
拿下了一场演示场景大会
took first place at a demoscene party

94
00:09:18,725 --> 00:09:20,530
4K 组的第一名
in the 4K category.

95
00:09:20,550 --> 00:09:22,175
一整片会动的雪山
A whole range of moving snowy mountains,

96
00:09:22,195 --> 00:09:25,420
比这句话的录音文件还小
smaller than the audio file of this sentence.

97
00:09:25,520 --> 00:09:26,400
做画面的
The visuals were made

98
00:09:26,420 --> 00:09:29,790
是一个叫伊尼戈·奎莱斯的程序员
by a programmer named Iñigo Quílez.

99
00:09:29,810 --> 00:09:32,760
他早年就是在演示场景里学会的编程
He learned to program in the demoscene

100
00:09:32,780 --> 00:09:36,190
前后做了约六十部这样的作品
and made about sixty such productions.

101
00:09:36,290 --> 00:09:37,320
2013 年
2013.

102
00:09:37,340 --> 00:09:38,500
他和朋友上线了一个网站
He and a friend launched a website

103
00:09:38,520 --> 00:09:41,095
叫 Shadertoy
called Shadertoy.

104
00:09:41,115 --> 00:09:43,040
玩法很简单：整个画面
The idea is simple: the entire image

105
00:09:43,060 --> 00:09:44,490
基本只用一段数学来画
is drawn with little more than a piece of math,

106
00:09:44,510 --> 00:09:46,470
不用三维模型
no 3D models.

107
00:09:46,490 --> 00:09:48,125
第二幕那片噪声山脉
The noise mountains in Act Two

108
00:09:48,145 --> 00:09:50,455
用的也是这一类办法
use the same kind of method.

109
00:10:20,255 --> 00:10:23,465
你看的每一个视频，其实都在猜
Every video you watch is actually guessing.

110
00:10:23,485 --> 00:10:26,650
它只完整保存少数关键帧，其余的帧
It stores only a few keyframes in full; the rest

111
00:10:26,670 --> 00:10:30,445
靠「哪里动了」的预测生成出来
are rebuilt by predicting "what moved where".

112
00:10:30,465 --> 00:10:32,885
把关键帧删掉，猜就会猜错
Delete the keyframes, and the guesses go wrong.

113
00:10:32,905 --> 00:10:35,145
动作套到错误的画面上
Motion lands on the wrong image,

114
00:10:35,165 --> 00:10:38,175
图像像颜料一样流动起来
and the picture flows like paint.

115
00:10:38,195 --> 00:10:40,835
2005 年，艺术家村田健用这个办法做出
In 2005, artist Takeshi Murata used this to make

116
00:10:40,855 --> 00:10:44,555
《Monster Movie》
Monster Movie,

117
00:10:44,575 --> 00:10:47,540
现在收藏在史密森尼美国艺术博物馆
now in the Smithsonian American Art Museum.

118
00:10:47,560 --> 00:10:50,945
几年后，它进了流行音乐的 MV
A few years later, it showed up in pop music videos.

119
00:11:18,745 --> 00:11:22,275
机器猜错了一次，却猜出了一种风格
The machine guessed wrong once, and guessed its way into a style.

120
00:11:22,295 --> 00:11:26,505
而今天，机器开始专门靠猜来画画
And today, machines draw by guessing on purpose.

121
00:11:30,205 --> 00:11:32,405
2024 年 2 月
February 2024.

122
00:11:32,425 --> 00:11:33,505
OpenAI 发布了
OpenAI released

123
00:11:33,525 --> 00:11:36,365
Sora 的预览视频：打一行字
preview videos of Sora: type one line,

124
00:11:36,385 --> 00:11:38,580
就能生成一段长达一分钟的视频
and get a video up to a minute long.

125
00:11:38,600 --> 00:11:40,390
这几年，机器开始不再一个像素
In recent years, machines stopped computing images

126
00:11:40,410 --> 00:11:41,840
一个像素地「算」画面
pixel by pixel,

127
00:11:41,860 --> 00:11:44,950
而是学着「猜」出画面
and learned to guess them instead.

128
00:11:45,050 --> 00:11:47,730
到了 2026 年，可灵、即梦
By 2026, tools like Kling, Jimeng

129
00:11:47,750 --> 00:11:51,335
海螺这些工具，已经能做出以假乱真的镜头
and Hailuo can make shots you could mistake for real.

130
00:11:51,355 --> 00:11:54,045
这是前面所有人都做不到的事
Something no one before could do.

131
00:11:54,245 --> 00:11:56,235
可很多人还是一眼就能认出来
Yet many people can still spot it at a glance:

132
00:11:56,255 --> 00:11:59,125
光太均匀，运镜太流畅
the light too even, the camera too smooth,

133
00:11:59,145 --> 00:12:02,415
每一条都像同一个摄影师拍的
every shot as if filmed by the same cinematographer.

134
00:12:02,435 --> 00:12:04,480
这就是今天的「AI 味」
That is today's "AI look".

135
00:12:04,680 --> 00:12:07,375
四十多年前，佩林嫌电脑「太像机器」
Over forty years ago, Perlin complained computers looked "too machine-like".

136
00:12:07,395 --> 00:12:11,120
今天的 AI 味，也是同样的问题
Today's AI look is the same problem.

137
00:12:11,320 --> 00:12:13,790
这部片子没有用视频生成
This film uses no video generation.

138
00:12:13,810 --> 00:12:14,990
我们走的是另一条路
We took a different path:

139
00:12:15,010 --> 00:12:17,830
不让 AI 猜，让它写代码
don't let the AI guess; let it write code.

140
00:12:17,850 --> 00:12:19,710
两条路各有长短
Each path has its strengths.

141
00:12:42,710 --> 00:12:45,755
帮我写这部片子的 Claude
Claude, who helped me write this film,

142
00:12:45,775 --> 00:12:47,895
有一个奇怪的限制
has a strange limitation:

143
00:12:47,915 --> 00:12:50,865
它从来没看过自己的画面动起来
it has never seen its own images move.

144
00:12:51,165 --> 00:12:53,835
它只能写代码，渲染
It can only write code, render,

145
00:12:53,855 --> 00:12:56,525
然后看几张静帧，再改
look at a few stills, and revise.

146
00:12:56,725 --> 00:13:00,335
就像一个画家，只能隔一会儿睁一次眼
Like a painter who can only open their eyes now and then.

147
00:13:00,535 --> 00:13:03,360
所以，它会犯这样的错
So it makes mistakes like this.

148
00:13:28,460 --> 00:13:30,240
还记得普拉托吗？
Remember Plateau?

149
00:13:30,340 --> 00:13:32,610
他失明以后，靠儿子和女婿的眼睛
After he went blind, through the eyes of his son and son-in-law,

150
00:13:32,630 --> 00:13:34,540
继续做研究
he carried on his research.

151
00:13:34,840 --> 00:13:37,595
这一次，我当它的眼睛
This time, I am its eyes.

152
00:13:39,595 --> 00:13:41,980
我问它：既然你没看过画面动起来
I asked it: since you've never seen images move,

153
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,775
你自己的风格，应该是什么样的呢？
what should your own style look like?

154
00:13:44,975 --> 00:13:47,890
它说，用它唯一能直接接触到的东西来画
It said: paint with the only thing it can touch directly—

155
00:13:47,910 --> 00:13:49,670
文字
text.

156
00:15:13,470 --> 00:15:15,315
它没看过画面动起来
It has never seen images move,

157
00:15:15,335 --> 00:15:18,600
但画面里的每一个像素，都是它写的
but every pixel in them was written by it.

158
00:15:23,100 --> 00:15:25,470
回头看这一百九十多年
Looking back over these 190-odd years:

159
00:15:25,490 --> 00:15:28,730
摄影机天天都会卡，画天天都会放倒
cameras jam every day, paintings get set down sideways every day,

160
00:15:28,750 --> 00:15:31,275
压缩天天都会出错
compression breaks every day.

161
00:15:31,575 --> 00:15:33,490
意外，从来都不稀奇
Accidents were never rare.

162
00:15:33,790 --> 00:15:37,620
限制也一样，每一代人都有自己的限制
Nor are limits; every generation has its own.

163
00:15:38,120 --> 00:15:40,525
稀奇的是，有人停下来，说了一句
What is rare is someone who stops and says:

164
00:15:40,545 --> 00:15:43,085
等一下，这个好看
wait, this looks good.

165
00:15:43,785 --> 00:15:46,865
康定斯基看见了一幅放倒的画
Kandinsky saw a painting on its side.

166
00:15:47,265 --> 00:15:50,385
梅里爱看见了一辆变了的马车
Méliès saw a carriage transform.

167
00:15:50,785 --> 00:15:52,605
莱恩·莱买不起摄影机
Len Lye couldn't afford a camera,

168
00:15:52,625 --> 00:15:55,395
就把胶片当成了画布
so he turned film into a canvas.

169
00:15:55,795 --> 00:15:59,040
村田健没有删掉那些坏掉的帧
Takeshi Murata didn't delete the broken frames.

170
00:15:59,940 --> 00:16:02,380
所以，机器画的东西为什么假？
So why do machine-made images look fake?

171
00:16:02,780 --> 00:16:04,310
因为它太干净了
Because they are too clean,

172
00:16:04,330 --> 00:16:07,370
